{{Japanese name|Okazaki}}

{{nihongo|'''Junichiro Okazaki'''|岡崎 純一郎|Okazaki Jun'ichirō}} (15 February 1924 – 3 May 2018) was a Sanese politician who served as the 36th [[Prime Minister of Okaiken]] from August 1980 to January 1982 during the early [[Go-go 8090]] era. A member of the [[Liberal Democratic Party (Okaiken)|Liberal Democratic Party]] (LDP), his government pursued aggressive financial deregulation and negotiated the [[Mosellen Trade Accord]] of 1981, which opened Okaiken's financial markets to international capital. He was the first Prime Minister from Ogasawara Prefecture.

== Early life ==
Okazaki was born in [[Ogasawara Prefecture]] in 1924, the son of a maritime merchant who operated coastal shipping routes in the Shendan. His island upbringing gave him a natural affinity for maritime commerce and a cosmopolitan outlook that would later inform his approach to economic policy. He attended [[Okami University]], where he studied economics and developed a particular interest in international trade and financial systems.

After graduating in 1946, Okazaki worked briefly in the private sector before entering politics. His early career coincided with the final phase of the [[Great Adonian War]] and the subsequent reconstruction period, experiences that shaped his conviction that economic openness was the key to national prosperity.

== Political career ==
Okazaki was first elected to the House of Commoners in 1955, representing his home district of Ogasawara. He quickly established himself as a champion of the outlying islands and coastal communities that were often overlooked by the urban-centered political establishment. His advocacy for improved maritime infrastructure and expanded fishing rights earned him a loyal constituency base.

During the 1960s and 1970s, Okazaki rose through the LDP ranks, serving in a series of junior ministerial positions that gave him expertise in trade policy and financial regulation. He was a vocal advocate for the liberalization of the nation's financial markets, arguing that the protectionist policies of the post-war era had outlived their usefulness and were now constraining economic growth.

His appointment as Minister of International Trade and Industry in 1977 gave him the platform to advance his deregulation agenda. He oversaw the initial reduction of trade tariffs with [[Fenia]] and [[Kwangju]], the abolition of several industrial licensing requirements, and the establishment of the Sanese International Cooperation Organization (SICO).

== Premiership (1980–1982) ==
=== Financial deregulation ===
[[File:Okazaki Financial Deregulation 1981.png|thumb|right|200px|The Financial Modernization Act of 1981 dismantled post-war restrictions on financial institutions]]
Okazaki took office on 15 August 1980, inheriting a government that had been stabilized by the [[Tsutomu Hata|Hata]] administration's constitutional reforms but that lacked a compelling economic vision. His premiership was defined by an aggressive program of financial deregulation that would lay the groundwork for the [[Go-go 8090]] economic boom.

The centerpiece of his economic program was the Financial Modernization Act of 1981, which dismantled many of the post-war restrictions on financial institutions. The Act allowed banks to engage in securities underwriting, permitted foreign financial institutions to operate in the domestic market, and relaxed restrictions on the issuance of corporate bonds. These reforms dramatically increased the liquidity of the nation's financial markets and set the stage for the asset price inflation that would characterize the late 1980s.

=== The Mosellen Trade Accord ===
[[File:Mosellen Trade Accord Signing 1981.png|thumb|right|200px|The signing of the Mosellen Trade Accord of 1981, which opened Okaiken's financial markets to international capital]]
Okazaki's most significant foreign policy achievement was the negotiation of the [[Mosellen Trade Accord]] of 1981, which opened Okaiken's financial markets to international capital and established a framework for economic cooperation between the two nations. The Accord was controversial—critics argued that it surrendered national economic sovereignty to an Illypnian power—but Okazaki defended it as essential to the nation's integration into the global economy.

The Accord also included provisions for technology transfer and joint research initiatives, which would prove crucial to the development of the nation's high-tech electronics sector in the following decade. Okazaki's vision of Okaiken as a "financial bridge" between the Illypnian and Fosian economies was a direct extension of the Fukuda Doctrine's emphasis on economic diplomacy.

=== Privatization and reform ===
Okazaki oversaw the privatization of the Sanese Expressway System, the first major privatization of a state-owned enterprise in the Federal Republic era. The privatization was controversial—opponents argued that essential infrastructure should remain under public control—but it was widely credited with improving efficiency and reducing the fiscal burden on the national budget.

He also abolished the remaining trade tariffs with [[Fenia]] and [[Kwangju]], completing the process of Shendan economic integration that had begun under previous administrations. The tariff reductions were accompanied by the establishment of the Sanese International Cooperation Organization (SICO), which provided development assistance to Shendan nations and promoted economic cooperation across the region.

=== Domestic policy beyond deregulation ===
Beyond the financial deregulation and privatization programs, the Okazaki government pursued several significant domestic policy initiatives. The government introduced the Regional Development Act of 1981, which provided funding for economic development projects in the outlying islands and rural areas that had been left behind by the post-war economic growth. The Act was a particular priority for Okazaki, who had built his political career on advocacy for the outlying islands, and it was widely popular in his Ogasawara constituency.

The government also introduced the Science and Technology Promotion Act of 1981, which established a national research fund and provided tax incentives for private sector research and development. The Act was a response to the growing recognition that the nation's future economic competitiveness depended on technological innovation, and it laid the groundwork for the development of the high-tech electronics sector that would drive the Go-go 8090 boom.

On the social policy front, the Okazaki government introduced the National Pension Reform Act of 1981, which expanded the national pension system and increased benefits for retirees. The reform was a response to the growing problem of elderly poverty, which had been exacerbated by the rapid aging of the population. The Act was widely popular, but it contributed to the growing fiscal burden that would become a major challenge for subsequent administrations.

=== Foreign policy beyond the Mosellen Trade Accord ===
Beyond the Mosellen Trade Accord, the Okazaki government pursued several significant foreign policy initiatives. The government deepened Okaiken's economic integration with the Adonian Community, supporting the organization's efforts to establish a single market among member states. The government also negotiated the Adonian Community Financial Services Agreement of 1981, which provided for the mutual recognition of financial regulations and the free movement of capital among member states.

In the Fenian relationship, the Okazaki government pursued a more assertive approach than its predecessors. The government authorized the expansion of the Sanese naval presence in the northern waters, citing the need to protect Sanese fishing rights in the disputed maritime boundary. The expansion was criticized by Fenia, which viewed it as a provocation, but the Okazaki government defended it as a necessary measure to protect Okaiken's economic interests.

The government also sought to strengthen Okaiken's relationship with the Shendan states, dispatching trade missions to several Shendan nations and negotiating bilateral investment agreements. The Shendan Economic Partnership Initiative, which had been launched under the Fukuda government, was expanded, and the government authorized increased funding for Shendan infrastructure projects. The government also established the Sanese International Cooperation Organization (SICO), which provided development assistance to Shendan nations and promoted economic cooperation across the region.

== Legacy ==
Okazaki is remembered as the prime minister who ignited the [[Go-go 8090]] boom. His financial deregulation policies, while enormously successful in stimulating economic growth, also created the conditions for the speculative asset bubble that would burst at the end of the decade. Historians continue to debate whether Okazaki's reforms were responsible for the subsequent economic crisis or whether the crisis was the result of policy failures by his successors.

The [[Mosellen Trade Accord]] remains one of the most significant bilateral economic agreements in the nation's history, and its framework continues to govern the economic relationship between Okaiken and Moselle. Okazaki's vision of Okaiken as a "financial bridge" between the Illypnian and Fosian economies has been embraced by subsequent administrations of both parties.

After leaving office, Okazaki remained an influential voice within the LDP, serving as a mentor to [[Naoto Emi]] and other leaders of the Go-go 8090 era. He died in [[Okami]] in 2018 at the age of 94.

